A||||4||Carrolles||MSD Ignition||||1
Z||000000||Carrolles||09-22-1999||05:54 PM||||Is anyone using an MSD 6A ignition system?  I'm installing a newly rebuilt v-6 (.040 overbore) and am considering using one with the MSD High output coil and wiring harness.<P>I've gasket match ported the intake manifold to the plenum and the plenum mating parts and I have re-welded and ported the exhaust manifolds.  Since I bought a warranted long block I can't port the heads without voiding the warranty.  I wonder what overall increase I'll see over my old tired v-6.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Cooter||09-22-1999||08:11 PM||||I have a 6al and GM blaster coil on my Iron Duck and I think it does alright. THe car gets around 30 mpg and has never given any problems from the ignition. I also have a 6a on my 454 suburban and that thing is a monster. Of course, it has a lot more goodies in the engine that probably helps.||||reg||1
Z||000002||mwbackus||09-22-1999||08:26 PM||||I have been very happy with my MSD setup (6A with GM Blaster coil).  I mounted the control unit in the trunk (or whatever that space is rear of the motor), to help ensure that heat would not be a problem.  The 8876 wiring harness makes for a fast and easy install.  There was a "significant" improvement in higher RPM performance with this system, well worth its cost.  Unsure about any gas milleage increase, dont really care. ;-)<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000003||Patrick||09-23-1999||01:47 AM||||<BR>Hey Mike, I'm just curious. With absolutely no air circulation in the trunk, is it necessarily cooler in there than in the engine compartment? Especially if one has a black Fiero and the sun is beating down?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||mwbackus||09-23-1999||05:29 AM||||     I would say that adequate heat dissipation is much more important factor than the air circulation encountered within the engine compartment. eg.. blowing hot air on a mass will not cool it significantly if the mass is already cooler than this air :-).<BR>     The ambient air temperature in the trunk will nearly always be much lower than the unit itself during operation, since the avg. surface temp of the 6A cabinet can easily exceed 140 degrees F.  Therefore, adequate heat convection should occur until a temperature equilibrium is reached.  <BR>     The only time that the sun beating down on your car could significantly effect the temperature of the trunk, is when the car sits for a "considerable" period of time.  But, since the construction material used for the hatch, wing, etc. is not a good conductor of this heat into our faily well insulated trunks, the temperature would still not even approach the average temperature of the engine compartment while the motor is running.  A common misconception about our cars is that your luggage will melt if placed in the trunk during long driving vacations, etc.. LOL<BR>     The extreme temperature in the Fiero engine compartment encountered while the engine is at an idle (ie..stop lights, traffic, etc.) is the one of the reasons that the ECM is mounted in the cockpit, and one would assume that this may be another very good place to mount a device such as the 6A.  But alas, the LAST thing you want to do is move a source of extremely high electromagnetic emission to the proximity of your Fieros computer, radio, etc..  <P>Mike<BR>||||reg||1
